      "location": "Statement and Proof step 1.1",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "`def-computable-and-partial-computable-function` begins by fixing encodings, and `def-partial-function-computed-by-a-machine` defines computation on input `e_X(x)`. But step 1.1 says to choose a machine F that computes f “on binary numerals” and hard-wires `bin(n)`, without assuming that the fixed encoding of N is binary numerals or that translations are computable. `def-effective-binary-encoding-and-decoder` expressly says its effectiveness “does not yet formalize computation by Turing machines.” Thus [L1] does not license the constructed P_n; the theorem needs a standard/computably intertranslatable numeral-encoding hypothesis.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "a420948ca2dd260a042214091708673cb074584a21cd713708e32a49fd5f1e79"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:11:2",
      "id": "thm-halting-is-recognizable-and-undecidable",
      "location": "Facts & Assumptions [L2], used in Proof step 1.1",
      "defect": "citation-inaccurate",
      "evidence": "`def-universal-turing-machine` requires a universal U to halt when M halts “with output u” and explicitly states: “No condition is imposed” when M halts with no output word under its strict output convention. `thm-existence-of-a-universal-turing-machine` asserts only existence of a universal in that weaker sense. By `def-halting-computation-and-divergence`, HALT includes every accepting or rejecting history, with no output requirement; e.g. a machine can halt after leaving cell 0 blank and writing a nonblank symbol at cell 1, which has no output word by that definition. The cited theorem therefore does not yield [L2]'s claim that U halts exactly when M halts, so the recognizability inference is unlicensed.",
      "severity": "fatal",
